# Netwise -- line endings, decided here rather than per machine.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   Without it, what lands in the index depends on each person's local
#   `core.autocrlf`, which nobody agreed and nobody can see. Measured on this
#   repository before adding it:
#
#       71 files   i/lf  w/crlf     (this machine: core.autocrlf=true)
#        7 files   i/lf  w/lf
#
#   The index is uniformly LF today -- but only because the one person
#   committing happens to have autocrlf on. A teammate with it unset, which is
#   the default on macOS and Linux, commits CRLF instead. The result is a diff
#   showing every line of a file changed when one line did.
#
#   That is worse here than the usual tidiness argument. A whole-file diff is
#   exactly where a real change hides: two guard tests in this repository were
#   truncated during a conflict resolution and asserted nothing for days,
#   because nobody re-read a diff line by line. Anything that inflates diffs
#   makes that class of accident more likely, not less.
#
#   This is the same shape as the ruff pin (#84): a guarantee that held against
#   an unstated condition -- whichever config happened to be set that day --
#   rather than something the repository enforces.
#
# WHAT IT DOES
#   `text=auto eol=lf` normalises every text file to LF *in the index*, while
#   leaving working trees alone. Windows editors keep seeing CRLF locally; git
#   stores LF for everyone. Nobody has to change a personal setting, which is
#   the point -- a convention that needs each person to remember is the kind
#   this project keeps finding broken.
#
#   Adding this is a no-op for the current tree: the index is already all LF,
#   verified with `git ls-files --eol` before committing, so it introduces no
#   renormalisation churn.

* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Explicitly text, so intent is readable rather than inferred -----------
*.py        text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.cfg       text eol=lf
*.xml       text eol=lf
.gitignore  text eol=lf
Modelfile   text eol=lf

# --- Binary: never touched, never diffed as text ---------------------------
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.ico       binary
*.zip       binary
*.docx      binary
*.xlsx      binary
*.pptx      binary

# --- Fixtures are the product's input, and their bytes matter --------------
#   A converted PF Sense config is compared against a hand-written Cisco one,
#   and tests assert on exact ACL line content. Marking them text eol=lf keeps
#   that comparison stable across machines rather than depending on checkout.
tests/fixtures/**  text eol=lf
